BOOK = {'title': 'The Ugly Duckling', 'author': 'Hans Christian Andersen', 'genre': "Andersen's Fairy Tales", 'chapters': [{'n': 1, 'title': 'Complete Text', 'text': "It was so glorious out in the country; it was summer; the wheatfields were golden, the oatfields green, and the hay was stacked in the meadows. The stork was walking about on his long red legs, chattering in Egyptian, for that is the language he had learned from his mother. All around the fields and meadows were great woods, and in the midst of the woods deep lakes. Yes, it was indeed glorious out in the country. In the midst of the sunshine there lay an old manor house, with deep canals round it, and from the wall down to the water there were great burdocks, so high that little children could stand upright under the loftiest of them. It was just as wild there as in the deepest wood, and here sat a Duck upon her nest. She had to hatch her ducklings; but she was almost tired out before the little ones came; and she sat so long on them, that they at last hatched. One after another, the little ducks came creeping out. 'Quack! quack!' said they, and they all came out as fast as they could, looking all around them under the green leaves; and the mother let them look as much as they liked, for green is good for the eyes. 'How large the world is!' said the young ones, for they certainly had much more room now than when they were in the eggs. 'Do you think this is all the world?' said the mother. 'Oh, it extends far beyond the other side of the garden, quite into the parson's field; but I have never been there. Are you all here?' and she stood up. 'No, not all; the largest egg is still there. How long will this last? I am quite tired of it.' And she sat down again. 'Well, how are you getting on?' asked an old Duck, who had come to pay her a visit. 'There's one egg that takes a long time,' said the Duck who sat there; 'it will not break. But now let us look at the others. They are the prettiest little ducks I ever saw. They are all like their father; the rogue, he never comes to see me.' 'Let me see the egg that will not break,' said the old Duck. 'You may be sure that it is a turkey's egg. I was once cheated in the same way, and I had great anxiety and trouble with the young ones, for they are afraid of the water. I could not get them to venture in. I quacked and clucked, but it was no use. Let me see the egg. Yes, that is a turkey's egg. Leave it alone, and teach the other children to swim.' 'I think I will sit on it a little longer,' said the Duck. 'I have sat so long already that I may as well sit a few days more.' 'Just as you please,' said the old Duck; and she went away. At last the great egg broke. 'Pip! pip!' said the young one, and crept out. It was very large and ugly. The Duck looked at it. 'That is a very great, ugly duckling,' said she; 'it does not look at all like the others: can it really be a turkey chick? Well, we shall soon find out. It must go into the water, even if I have to push it in.' The next day the weather was splendid, and the sun shone brightly on the green burdocks. The mother Duck with all her family went down to the canal. Splash! she jumped into the water. 'Quack! quack!' she said, and one duckling after another plunged in. The water closed over their heads, but they came up again in an instant, and swam beautifully; their legs went of themselves, and they were all in the water, even the ugly grey one. 'No, it is not a turkey,' said the old Duck; 'see how nicely it uses its legs, and how upright it holds itself. It is my own child! On the whole it is quite pretty, when you look at it properly. Quack! quack! now come with me, and I will take you into the world, and introduce you in the duck-yard; but keep close to me, so that no one may tread on you, and take care of the cats.' So they came to the duck-yard. There was a terrible noise there, for two families were quarreling about an eel's head, and in the end it was carried off by the cat. 'See, that is the way of the world,' said the mother Duck, and she whetted her beak, for she too wanted the eel's head. 'Only use your legs,' she said. 'See that you can bustle about, and bow your heads before the old Duck yonder. She is the most distinguished of all here; she is of Spanish blood, and that is why she is so fat; and do you see, she has a red rag round her leg; that is considered extremely fine, and is the greatest distinction a duck can have. Don't turn in your toes; a well-bred duckling spreads his feet wide apart, just like father and mother, so. Now bend your necks, and say 'quack.'' And they did so; but the other ducks round about looked at them, and said quite loud: 'Look there! now we are to have another brood, as if there were not enough of us already; and — fie! — how ugly that one is! We will not endure that!' And immediately one of the ducks flew at him, and bit him in the neck. 'Let him alone,' said the mother; 'he is not doing any one any harm.' 'Yes, but he is so large and so strange,' said the duck who had bitten him; 'and therefore he must be turned out.' 'Those are pretty children that the old one has,' said the old Duck with the rag round her leg. 'They are all pretty except one, and that has not turned out well. I wish she could hatch it over again.' 'That cannot be done, my lady,' said the mother Duck. 'It is true that he is not handsome, but he is a very good child, and swims as well as the others, indeed a little better. I think he will grow handsome, and become smaller in time. He stayed too long in the egg, and therefore is not properly formed.' And then she scratched his neck and smoothed his feathers. 'Moreover, he is a drake,' she said, 'and therefore it does not matter so much. I think he will be very strong: he is getting on already.' 'The other ducklings are graceful enough,' said the old Duck. 'Make yourself at home, and if you find an eel's head, be so kind as to bring it to me.' So they made themselves at home. But the poor duckling, who had come last out of the egg, and who was so ugly, was bitten and pushed and made fun of, as much by the ducks as by the chickens. 'He is too big!' they all said. And the turkey-cock, who had been born with spurs, and therefore thought himself an emperor, puffed himself up like a ship with sails, and walked up to him, and gobbled, and grew quite red in the face. The poor duckling did not know where he dared to stand or walk; he was quite miserable because he was so ugly, and was the jest of the whole duck-yard. So it went on the first day, and afterwards it became worse and worse. The poor duckling was chased and buffeted about by all; even his brothers and sisters behaved unkindly to him, and were always saying: 'May the cat take you, you ugly creature!' And the mother said: 'I wish you were far away!' And the ducks bit him, and the chickens pecked him, and the girl who fed the poultry kicked him with her foot. Then he ran and flew over the fence, and the little birds in the bushes flew up in alarm. 'That is because I am so ugly,' thought the duckling, and shut his eyes, but ran on still. At last he came to a great moor, where the wild ducks lived. There he lay the whole night, and he was very tired and sorrowful. In the morning the wild ducks came up, and looked at their new comrade. 'What sort of a duck are you?' they asked, and the duckling turned himself in all directions, and bowed to them in the best way he could. 'You are terribly ugly,' said the wild ducks, 'but that does not matter to us, if you do not marry into our family.' Poor thing! he had never thought of marrying; he only wanted leave to lie among the reeds and drink some of the water. He lay there two whole days; then came two wild geese, or rather two wild ganders. They had not been long out of the shell, and were very saucy. 'Hark ye, comrade,' said they; 'you are so ugly that we like you very well. Will you go with us, and become a bird of passage? Not far from here, in another moor, there are some pretty wild geese, all unmarried, and all able to say 'Honk!' You are ugly enough to make your fortune among them.' 'Bang!' went a gun all at once, and both the wild ganders fell dead among the reeds, and the water became red with blood. 'Bang!' went a gun again, and whole flocks of wild geese flew up from the reeds. And then another bang went off. A great hunt was going on. The hunters lay in ambush all round the moor, and some were even sitting in the trees, and looking over the reeds. The blue smoke rose up like clouds among the dark trees, and was wafted far away over the water; and the hunting dogs came splash, splash, into the moor, and the reeds and the rushes bent down on every side. How frightened the poor little duckling was! He turned his head, and wanted to hide it under his wing, but at that moment a great terrible dog stood close by the duckling. His tongue was hanging out of his mouth, and his eyes gleamed horribly. He thrust his nose close to the duckling, showed his sharp teeth, and then, splash, splash! he went on, without seizing him. 'Oh, Heaven be thanked!' sighed the duckling. 'I am so ugly that even the dog will not bite me!' And so he lay quite still, while the shot rattled through the reeds, and gun after gun went off. It was late in the day before it became quiet; but the poor little thing did not dare to get up. He waited several hours more before he looked round him, and then hastened away from the moor as fast as he could. He ran over fields and meadows, and there was such a wind that he had hard work to make his way. Towards evening he reached a poor little hut. It was so dilapidated that it did not know on which side to fall, and that is why it remained standing. The wind whistled round the duckling in such a way that he had to sit down to hold himself, and it became worse and worse. Then he noticed that the door had lost one of its hinges, and hung so crookedly that he could creep through the crack into the room. An old woman lived there with her cat and her hen. The cat, which she called her little son, could arch his back, and purr, and give out sparks when he was stroked the wrong way. The hen had quite short legs, and was therefore called 'Chickie-short-legs'; she laid good eggs, and the old woman loved her as her own child. In the morning the strange duckling was at once noticed, and the cat began to purr, and the hen to cluck. 'What is that?' said the old woman, and looked round; but her eyes were not good, and she thought the duckling was a fat duck that had strayed. 'That is a capital capture!' said she. 'I hope it is not a drake. We must see.' So the duckling was taken on trial for three weeks, but no eggs came. And the cat was master of the house, and the hen was mistress, and they always said: 'We and the world!' for they thought they were half the world, and by far the better half. The duckling thought one might have a different opinion, but the hen would not allow it. 'Can you lay eggs?' she asked. 'No.' 'Then you will please hold your tongue.' And the cat said: 'Can you arch your back and purr and give out sparks?' 'No.' 'Then you had better not have an opinion when sensible people are speaking.' And the duckling sat in a corner and was in low spirits; then he began to think of the fresh air and the sunshine, and was seized with a strange longing to swim on the water, so that he could not help telling the hen about it. 'What is the matter with you?' she asked. 'You have nothing to do, and that is why you get these fancies. Either lay eggs or purr, and you will get over them.' 'But it is so delightful to swim on the water,' said the duckling, 'and so refreshing to feel it close over your head when you dive to the bottom.' 'That would be a great pleasure indeed!' said the hen. 'I think you have gone mad. Ask the cat about it — he is the wisest animal I know — ask him if he likes to swim on the water or to dive under it. I say nothing about myself. Ask our mistress, the old woman; no one in the world is wiser than she. Do you think she would like to swim and let the water close over her head?' 'You do not understand me,' said the duckling. 'We do not understand you! Then who is to understand you? Do you want to be wiser than the cat and the old woman? I will not say anything of myself. Don't be so conceited, child, and thank your Maker for all the kindness you have received. Are you not in a warm room, and have you not society around you from which you can learn something? But you are a chatterer, and it is not pleasant to have to do with you. You may believe me, I speak for your good. I tell you disagreeable things, and by them you may know your friends. Only take care that you learn to lay eggs, or to purr and give out sparks.' 'I think I will go out into the wide world again,' said the duckling. 'Yes, do go,' said the hen. So the duckling went away. He swam on the water, and dived under it, but he was slighted by every creature because he was so ugly. Now came the autumn; the leaves in the forest turned yellow and brown; the wind caught them so that they danced about, and the air was very cold. The clouds hung low, heavy with hail and snow, and the raven stood on the fence and croaked. The poor duckling was certainly not in good spirits from the cold. One evening, just as the sun was setting with unusual brilliancy, a flock of beautiful large birds came out of the bushes. The duckling had never seen anything so beautiful. They were dazzlingly white, with long, graceful necks. They were swans. They uttered a singular cry, spread out their magnificent broad wings, and flew away from the cold regions to warmer lands, to open lakes. They mounted so high, so high! And the ugly little duckling felt quite strangely as he watched them. He turned round and round in the water like a wheel, stretched out his neck towards them, and uttered such a loud and strange cry that he frightened himself. Oh! he could not forget them, those beautiful, happy birds; and when he was out of sight of them, he dived to the very bottom, and when he came up again he was quite beside himself. He did not know what the birds were, nor whither they flew; but he loved them as he had never loved anything before. He did not envy them at all; how could he think of wishing to be so beautiful? He would have been glad if the ducks had only endured him among them, the poor, ugly creature! And the winter was so cold, so cold! The duckling had to swim about in the water to keep it from freezing; but every night the hole in which he swam became smaller and smaller. Then it froze so hard that the ice cracked; and the duckling had to use his legs to keep the hole open. At last he became exhausted, and lay quite still, and thus froze fast in the ice. Early in the morning a peasant came by, and when he saw what had happened, he broke the ice in pieces with his wooden shoe, and carried the duckling home to his wife. There it revived. The children wanted to play with it, but the duckling thought they wanted to hurt him, and in his fright jumped up into the milk-pan, so that the milk spurted out into the room. The woman clapped her hands, and he flew into the butter-tub, and then into the meal-barrel and out again. What a sight it was! The woman screamed, and struck at him with the fire-tongs; the children ran races to catch him, and laughed and shouted. Fortunately the door stood open, and the duckling flew out among the bushes and snow, and lay there in the snow, quite exhausted. But it would be too sad to tell all the misery and privation which the poor little duckling had to endure in the hard winter. He was lying on the moor among the reeds, when the sun began to shine warm again; the larks sang, and beautiful spring had come. Then all at once the duckling could flap his wings. They beat the air more strongly than before, and bore him away quickly, and before he well knew it, he was in a large garden, where the apple-trees stood in blossom, and the syringas scented the air and hung their long green branches down into the winding canal. Oh, it was so beautiful there, in the freshness of spring! And from the thicket came three beautiful white swans. They rustled their feathers, and swam lightly on the water. The duckling recognized the majestic birds, and was seized with a strange melancholy. 'I will fly to them, those royal birds, and they will kill me, because I, who am so ugly, dare to approach them. But it does not matter; better to be killed by them than to be bitten by the ducks, pecked by the chickens, kicked by the girl who feeds the poultry, and to have so much to suffer in winter!' So he flew into the water, and swam towards the beautiful swans. They saw him, and shot forward to meet him with ruffled feathers. 'Kill me!' said the poor bird, and bowed his head low, expecting death. But what did he see in the water? He saw his own image, and lo! it was no longer a clumsy, dark-grey bird, ugly and hateful to look at, but a graceful and beautiful swan! It does not matter that one has been born in a duck-yard, if one has only lain in a swan's egg. He felt quite glad that he had endured so much trouble and misfortune, for now he had a fuller understanding of his own happiness, and the large swans swam round him, and stroked him with their beaks. Some little children came into the garden with bread and corn, which they threw into the water; and the smallest cried out: 'There is a new one!' And the other children shouted with joy: 'Yes, a new one has come!' And they clapped their hands and danced about, and ran to their father and mother; and bread and cake were thrown into the water; and they all said: 'The new one is the most beautiful of all! So young and handsome!' And the old swans bowed their heads before him. Then he felt quite ashamed, and hid his head under his wing, for he did not know what to do. He was too happy, but not at all proud, for a good heart never becomes proud. He thought of how he had been persecuted and despised, and now he heard them all say that he was the most beautiful of all beautiful birds. And the syringas bent their branches down into the water before him, and the sun shone warm and mild. Then his wings rustled, his slender neck was raised, and from his heart he cried rejoicingly: 'I never dreamed of so much happiness when I was the ugly duckling!'"}]}

def word_count():
    return sum(len(c["text"].split()) for c in BOOK["chapters"])

def print_book(ch=None):
    print("=" * 64)
    print(BOOK["title"].upper(), "\nby", BOOK["author"], "-", BOOK["genre"])
    print("=" * 64)
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        if ch and c["n"] != ch:
            continue
        print("\n\nCHAPTER %d: %s\n%s" % (c["n"], c["title"], "-" * 40))
        print(c["text"])
    print("\n[%d words, %d chapters — complete]" % (word_count(), len(BOOK["chapters"])))

def search(word):
    w = word.lower()
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        for i, para in enumerate(c["text"].split("\n\n")):
            if w in para.lower():
                print("[ch %d, para %d] %s..." % (c["n"], i + 1, para[:160].replace("\n", " ")))

def write_html(path="book.html"):
    parts = ["<html><head><meta charset='utf-8'><title>%s</title></head><body>" % _h.escape(BOOK["title"])]
    parts.append("<h1>%s</h1><p><i>by %s — %s</i></p>" % (_h.escape(BOOK["title"]), _h.escape(BOOK["author"]), _h.escape(BOOK["genre"])))
    for c in BOOK["chapters"]:
        parts.append("<h2>Chapter %d: %s</h2>" % (c["n"], _h.escape(c["title"])))
        for para in c["text"].split("\n\n"):
            parts.append("<p>%s</p>" % _h.escape(para))
    parts.append("</body></html>")
    open(path, "w").write("\n".join(parts))
    print("wrote", path)

if __name__ == "__main__":
    a = sys.argv[1:]
    if a and a[0] == "html":
        write_html()
    elif len(a) >= 2 and a[0] == "search":
        search(a[1])
    elif a and a[0].isdigit():
        print_book(int(a[0]))
    else:
        print_book()

