en. There was once a bundle of matches, and these matches were very proud of their high descent. Their genealogical tree — that is to say, the great fir-tree, of which each of them was a little splinter — had been a great old tree in the wood. The matches now lay between a tinderbox and an old iron pot, and were telling about their youth. 'Yes, when we were on the green branches,' said they, 'then we were really on the green branches! Every morning and evening we had diamond tea — that is, dew; we had sunshine all day long, whenever the sun shone, and all the little birds had to tell us stories. We could see very well that we were rich, for the other trees were dressed only in summer, while our family could wear green clothes in winter as well as in summer. But then the woodcutter came, like a great revolution, and our family was broken up. The head of the family got a place as mainmast in a large ship, which could sail round the world if it liked; the other branches went to other places, and now we have the office of kindling a light for common use. That is why people of rank like us have come to the kitchen.' 'My fate has been different,' said the iron pot, by the side of which the matches lay. 'From the first moment I came into the world I have been scoured and cooked. I take care of what is solid, and am, in fact, the first in the house. My only pleasure is, after dinner, to take a clean and respectable place, and to sit and have a sensible conversation with my comrades. If we except the water-pail, which sometimes goes down into the yard, we live quite retired. Our only newsmonger is the market-basket; but he talks very disrespectfully about the government and the people. Yes, the other day an old pot was so alarmed that it fell down and broke in pieces. He is liberal, I can tell you!' 'Now you talk too much,' said the tinderbox, and the steel struck against the flint, so that sparks flew out. 'Shall we have a merry evening?' 'Yes, let us talk about who is of t