ched to the sea-shore. Poor little Eliza was standing in the peasant's room playing with a green leaf, for she had no other plaything. She pricked a hole in the leaf, and looked through it at the sun, and it seemed to her as if she saw her brothers' clear eyes; and each time the warm sun shone upon her cheeks, she thought of all the kisses they had given her. One day passed just like another. If the wind blew through the great rose-hedge before the house, it would whisper to the roses: 'Who is more beautiful than you?' But the roses would shake their heads and say: 'Eliza is.' And on Sunday, when the peasant's wife sat at the door reading her hymn-book, the wind would turn over the leaves and say to the book: 'Who is more pious and good than you?' 'Eliza is,' said the hymn-book; and the roses and the hymn-book told nothing but the truth. When she was fifteen years old, she returned home. But when the queen saw how beautiful she was, she became angry and envious, and wanted to make her as wicked and ugly as she herself was. First she smeared her with walnut-juice, so that she was quite brown, daubed her face with a nasty ointment, and tangled her beautiful hair. It was impossible to recognize pretty Eliza. When her father saw her, he was much frightened, and said she was not his daughter. No one but the watch-dog and the swallows knew her, and they were only poor animals and could say nothing. Then poor Eliza wept, and thought of her eleven brothers, who were all gone. Sorrowfully she crept out of the palace, and walked the whole day over fields and moors till she came to the great forest. She did not know whither she was going, but she felt so sad for her brothers, who, like herself, had been driven out into the world, that she was determined to seek them. Night came on, and she had quite lost her way. Then she lay down on the soft moss, said her evening prayer, and leaned her head against a stump. All was still and quiet in the wood, the air was so mild, and hundreds of glow-worms shone like green fire among the grass and the moss. When she awoke in the morning, and put aside the branches, she started; for close by she heard the rippling of water. There was a large lake with the most beautiful clear water in it. She bathed her face and hands in it, till she was fair and white again. Then she wandered on in the wood, not knowing whither she went. She saw the wild apples hanging on the trees, and ate of them to satisfy her hunger. Then she asked the reeds and the rushes which way her brothers had gone, but none of them knew. Towards evening she came to a peasant's hut; she peeped in at the window, and saw an old woman within. 'Good evening,' said Eliza to her; but the old woman only nodded, and said nothing. Then Eliza asked her the way to her brothers, but the old woman shook her head. All night Eliza dreamed of her brothers; they were all children again, playing together, writing with their golden pencils on the diamond slates, and looking at the beautiful picture-book which had cost half a kingdom. In her dream they were just as they used to be. When she awoke, the sun was high in the heavens. She could not see the old woman, so she went down to the water and bathed. Suddenly she heard the sound of swans' wings rustling in the air. She started up; the sun was just rising, and close by she saw eleven wild swans, with crowns on their heads, flying towards the land one behind the other, like a long white ribbon. Eliza ran down the slope to the shore, and hid herself behind the bushes. The swans alighted close by her, and flapped their great white wings. As soon as the sun had risen higher in the heavens, the swan's feathers suddenly fell off, and eleven handsome princes, her brothers, stood before her. She uttered a loud cry, for though they were much changed, she knew them, she felt sure they must be her brothers. And she sprang up and threw herself on their necks, and called them each by name. And the princes were delighted when they saw their little sister again, for they knew her, although she had grown tall and beautiful. They laughed and wept, and soon understood how wickedly their stepmother had behaved to them all. 'We brothers,' said the eldest, 'fly about as wild swans as long as the sun is in the heavens; but as soon as it sinks behind the hills, we recover our human shape. Therefore we must always be on the lookout for a safe resting-place for the night, for if we were flying towards the clouds when the sun set, we should fall down into the deep sea. We do not live here, but in a land just as fair, that lies beyond the sea; the way is long, we have to cross the great sea, and there is no island on our way where we could pass the night; we have only a little rock, just large enough for us to rest upon. If the sea is rough, the foam dashes over us; but we thank God even for this rock; we have spent whole nights there, weeping. Our youngest brother, who is still the most tender, cannot fly so far as we can, and therefore we take it in turns to carry him on our backs. But now we cannot stay here longer than to-morrow, for the sea is calm, and we must fly away.' 'Shall we ever be set free?' asked Eliza. 'Yes,' said her brothers, 'but on one condition. You must not speak or laugh for — shall we tell you? — for six years you must be dumb. And in that time you must sew for us eleven shirts of nettles. If a single word passes your lips before the work is finished, we shall die by it. Remember this.' And with these words they flew away. Eliza trembled, and her tears fell upon her hands. 'My dear brothers, I will do everything to set you free,' she whispered. She went into a churchyard, and gathered nettles, and carried them home. With her delicate hands she wove the nettles into flax, and then sewed the eleven shirts. She neither spoke nor laughed. A whole year passed, and she had finished one shirt. Then a king came hunting in the forest. He saw the beautiful dumb girl, and fell in lov